var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/lagrangian_neural_network/#lnn_tutorial","page":"Tutorial","text":"In this tutorial we learn the Lagrangian of the simplest mechanical system there is, the harmonic oscillator, in four ways: from accelerations with two losses, and from positions alone with two more. Because we know the true Lagrangian, we can judge each result. We will see that the right way to judge it is not to compare the learned Lagrangian with the true one.\n\nThe tutorial has five parts:\n\nthe system and the data,\nwhy a Lagrangian is not unique, and what we compare instead,\ntwo networks trained on accelerations,\ntwo networks trained on positions only,\na comparison of the four.","title":"Learning the Harmonic Oscillator"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/lagrangian_neural_network/#The-System","page":"Tutorial","text":"The harmonic oscillator has one position q, one velocity dotq, and the Lagrangian\n\nL(q dotq) = frac12left(dotq^2 - q^2right)\n\nIts Euler–Lagrange equation, fracddtpartial_dotqL = partial_qL, is ddotq = -q, whose solution from q(0) = q_0 and dotq(0) = dotq_0 is q(t) = q_0cos t + dotq_0sin t. The energy E = dotqpartial_dotqL - L is\n\nE(q dotq) = frac12left(dotq^2 + q^2right)\n\nand it is constant along each trajectory, so the trajectories are the circles E = const in the plane of (q dotq). The package stores a state as the vector x = q dotq, the position first:\n\nusing LagrangianNeuralNetworks\nusing GeometricMachineLearning: CPU, Adam, Batch, DataLoader, NeuralNetwork,\n                                TrainingOptimizer, train!\nusing CairoMakie\nimport Random\n\nE_true(x) = (x[1]^2 + x[2]^2) / 2  # the true energy of the state x = [q, q̇]\n∇E_true(x) = [x[1], x[2]]          # its gradient\nq_exact(t, q₀, q̇₀) = q₀ * cos(t) + q̇₀ * sin(t)\n\nThe networks trained on accelerations see 400 states drawn uniformly from the square -1 1^2, with the acceleration ddotq = -q at each:\n\nRandom.seed!(1234)\nX = 2 .* rand(2, 400) .- 1  # 2 × 400, the states [q; q̇]\nQ̈ = -X[1:1, :]              # 1 × 400, their accelerations\n\nWe judge each network on the 21 times 21 grid of the same square, and roll it out from q_0 = 08, dotq_0 = 0 for 100 steps of Deltat = 01, against the exact solution:\n\ngrid = hcat([[q, q̇] for q̇ in -1:0.1:1 for q in -1:0.1:1]...)  # 2 × 441\nQ̈_grid = -grid[1:1, :]\n\nconst Δt = 0.1\nN = 100\nq₀, q̇₀ = 0.8, 0.0\nQ_exact = [q_exact(k * Δt, q₀, q̇₀) for _ in 1:1, k in 0:N]  # 1 × (N + 1)","title":"The System"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/lagrangian_neural_network/#A-Lagrangian-Is-Not-Unique","page":"Tutorial","text":"Many Lagrangians give the equations of motion ddotq = -q. Multiplying L by a scale factor rho neq 0, adding a constant c, or adding a total time derivative fracddtF(q) = F(q)dotq does not change them:\n\ntildeL = rho L + c + fracddtF(q)\n\nhas the same Euler–Lagrange equations as L. In one dimension there is more: Lagrangians that are not of this form, inequivalent ones, give the same equations of motion too; Remark 3.3 of Offen [4], which excludes them, needs at least two dimensions. The data fix the dynamics, not the Lagrangian, and a network trained on the data learns one of these Lagrangians, which one depending on its initial parameters and its training. So we do not compare the learned L with the true one: a poor fit of the one to the other says nothing about the dynamics.\n\nWe compare what all of these Lagrangians share instead:\n\nthe acceleration they predict, ddotq(q dotq), on the grid;\nthe level sets of the energy: under the gauge above the energy becomes rho E - c, so its values change, but its level sets do not;\na rollout, the trajectory the learned model integrates from an initial condition.\n\nTo draw the level sets of a learned energy beside the true ones, we cannot use the same values. We take the levels through the same points (q 0) instead, which removes both rho and c:\n\nmatched_levels(E, qs) = [E([q, 0.0]) for q in qs]\nlevel_points = 0.2:0.2:1.0\nmatched_levels(E_true, level_points)\n5-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.020000000000000004\n 0.08000000000000002\n 0.18\n 0.32000000000000006\n 0.5\n\nEach of the four models below gives three functions — its acceleration on a matrix of states, its energy of a state, and its rollout — and one figure with three panels. The filled colours are the truth, and the dashed lines the learned model:\n\nqs = range(-1, 1; length = 41)\nstates = hcat([[q, q̇] for q̇ in qs for q in qs]...)\nacceleration_levels = range(-1, 1; length = 9)\n\nfunction plot_model(title, acceleration, energy, rollout)\n    fig = Figure(; size = (1200, 400))\n    Label(fig[0, 1:3], title; fontsize = 18)\n\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = L\"q\", ylabel = L\"\\dot{q}\", title = \"acceleration\")\n    contourf!(ax, qs, qs, [-q for q in qs, q̇ in qs]; levels = acceleration_levels)\n    contour!(ax, qs, qs, reshape(acceleration(states), length(qs), length(qs));\n        levels = acceleration_levels, color = :black, linestyle = :dash)\n\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, 2]; xlabel = L\"q\", ylabel = L\"\\dot{q}\", title = \"energy level sets\")\n    contourf!(ax, qs, qs, [E_true([q, q̇]) for q in qs, q̇ in qs];\n        levels = matched_levels(E_true, level_points), extendlow = :auto, extendhigh = :auto)\n    contour!(ax, qs, qs, [energy([q, q̇]) for q in qs, q̇ in qs];\n        levels = unique(sort(matched_levels(energy, level_points))),\n        color = :black, linestyle = :dash)\n\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, 3]; xlabel = L\"t\", ylabel = L\"q\", title = \"rollout\")\n    ts = Δt .* (0:N)\n    lines!(ax, ts, vec(Q_exact); label = \"exact\")\n    lines!(ax, ts, vec(rollout); color = :black, linestyle = :dash, label = \"learned\")\n    Legend(fig[2, 3], ax; orientation = :horizontal, framevisible = false)\n    fig\nend\n\nAll four networks have two hidden layers of width 4 and are trained in the same way, with Adam for 200 epochs in batches of 20:\n\nfunction train(arch, loss, input, output)\n    nn = NeuralNetwork(arch, CPU(), Float64)\n    dl = DataLoader(input, output; suppress_info = true)\n    optimizer = TrainingOptimizer(nn.params; algorithm = Adam(), linesearch = 1e-2)\n    train!(nn, optimizer, dl, Batch(20), 200; loss, show_progress = false)\n    nn\nend\n\nA continuous network gives the three functions directly: the package computes its acceleration, its energy as a function of (q dotq), which we wrap as a function of the state, and its rollout with the variational integrator of its midpoint discrete Lagrangian, variational_rollout, from the first two exact positions:\n\nq₁ = q_exact(Δt, q₀, q̇₀)\n\nfunction continuous_model(nn)\n    acceleration = LagrangianNeuralNetworks.lagrangian_acceleration(nn.architecture)\n    E = energy(nn)\n    (acceleration = x -> acceleration(x, nn.params),\n        energy = x -> E(x[1:1], x[2:2]),\n        rollout = variational_rollout(DiscreteLagrangian(nn, Δt), [q₀], [q₁], N))\nend","title":"A Lagrangian Is Not Unique"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/lagrangian_neural_network/#Training-on-Accelerations","page":"Tutorial","text":"","title":"Training on Accelerations"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/lagrangian_neural_network/#The-Lagrangian-Neural-Network","page":"Tutorial","text":"The loss of Cranmer et al. [1], LNNLoss, solves the Euler–Lagrange equation of the network for the acceleration and compares it with the data:\n\nRandom.seed!(1)\narch = LagrangianNeuralNetwork(2; width = 4)\nnn_lnn = train(arch, LNNLoss(arch), X, Q̈)\nmodel_lnn = continuous_model(nn_lnn)\nplot_model(\"LNNLoss\", model_lnn...)\nFigure()\n\nThe dashed lines follow the edges of the colours: the network predicts the acceleration, its energy has the circles as level sets, and its rollout follows the exact solution.","title":"The Lagrangian Neural Network"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/lagrangian_neural_network/#The-Euler–Lagrange-Residual","page":"Tutorial","text":"EulerLagrangeLoss asks the network to satisfy the Euler–Lagrange equation on the data, with no solve [4]. On its own, this loss is minimised by the zero Lagrangian, which satisfies every equation of motion. We therefore fix the gauge: a NormalisedLagrangian has the value 0 and the momentum 0 at the origin for every value of its parameters, which removes the constant and the linear gauge, and a ScaleCondition, added to the loss with RegularisedLoss, asks the velocity Hessian partial^2Lpartialdotq^2 to be 1 there, which removes the scale:\n\nRandom.seed!(2)\narch_normalised = NormalisedLagrangian(LagrangianNeuralNetwork(2; width = 4))\nloss = RegularisedLoss(EulerLagrangeLoss(arch_normalised), 1 => ScaleCondition(arch_normalised))\nnn_el = train(arch_normalised, loss, X, Q̈)\nmodel_el = continuous_model(nn_el)\nplot_model(\"EulerLagrangeLoss\", model_el...)\nFigure()","title":"The Euler–Lagrange Residual"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/lagrangian_neural_network/#Training-on-Positions-Only","page":"Tutorial","text":"Accelerations are rarely measured. A simulation or an experiment gives positions at discrete times. The two networks of this section see only triples of positions (q_k-1 q_k q_k+1), one timestep Deltat apart, on 400 trajectories q(t) = acos(t + varphi) of random amplitude a in 02 1 and phase varphi. The input of a sample is the pair q_k-1 q_k and its output q_k+1:\n\nRandom.seed!(1234)\na = 0.2 .+ 0.8 .* rand(400)\nφ = 2π .* rand(400)\npositions(k) = reshape(a .* cos.(φ .+ k * Δt), 1, :)\nposition_pairs = vcat(positions(0), positions(1))  # 2 × 400, [q_{k-1}; q_k]\nnext_positions = positions(2)                      # 1 × 400, q_{k+1}","title":"Training on Positions Only"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/lagrangian_neural_network/#The-Variational-Midpoint-Loss","page":"Tutorial","text":"VariationalMidpointLoss trains a continuous network through the discrete Euler–Lagrange equations of its midpoint discrete Lagrangian [5], plus its default regulariser, which penalises a degenerate discrete Lagrangian:\n\nRandom.seed!(1)\narch = LagrangianNeuralNetwork(2; width = 4)\nnn_vm = train(arch, VariationalMidpointLoss(arch, Δt), position_pairs, next_positions)\nmodel_vm = continuous_model(nn_vm)\nplot_model(\"VariationalMidpointLoss\", model_vm...)\nFigure()\n\nThis training does not learn the oscillator on every draw. Under Random.seed!(3) in place of Random.seed!(1), its acceleration_error is about 1, the error of the prediction ddotq = 0, and its rollout leaves the exact solution within the first period: the learned Lagrangian does not give the dynamics of the data.","title":"The Variational Midpoint Loss"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/lagrangian_neural_network/#The-Discrete-Lagrangian-Network","page":"Tutorial","text":"A DiscreteLagrangianNetwork learns the discrete Lagrangian L_d(q_0 q_1) itself [8], trained with DiscreteEulerLagrangeLoss. Its argument is the number of entries of a position, here 1:\n\nRandom.seed!(4)\narch_discrete = DiscreteLagrangianNetwork(1; width = 4)\nnn_del = train(arch_discrete, DiscreteEulerLagrangeLoss(arch_discrete, Δt), position_pairs,\n    next_positions)\n\nA discrete network has no velocity, so it has no acceleration and no energy of its own. The continuous Lagrangian it stands for is its inverse modified Lagrangian, L(q dotq) = L_d(q - Deltatdotq2 q + Deltatdotq2)Deltat, which inverse_modified_lagrangian returns as a function. We take its derivatives by central differences, and from them the acceleration ddotq = (L_q - L_qdotqdotq)L_dotqdotq, the energy E = dotqL_dotq - L and its gradient nabla E = (dotqL_qdotq - L_q dotqL_dotqdotq). The rollout uses the network itself as the discrete Lagrangian:\n\nfunction discrete_model(nn; h = 1e-4)\n    Lim = inverse_modified_lagrangian(nn, Δt)\n    L(q, v) = Lim([q], [v])\n    L_q(q, v) = (L(q + h, v) - L(q - h, v)) / 2h\n    L_v(q, v) = (L(q, v + h) - L(q, v - h)) / 2h\n    L_vv(q, v) = (L(q, v + h) - 2L(q, v) + L(q, v - h)) / h^2\n    L_qv(q, v) = (L(q + h, v + h) - L(q + h, v - h) - L(q - h, v + h) + L(q - h, v - h)) / 4h^2\n    q̈(q, v) = (L_q(q, v) - L_qv(q, v) * v) / L_vv(q, v)\n    (acceleration = x -> [q̈(x[1, b], x[2, b]) for _ in 1:1, b in axes(x, 2)],\n        energy = x -> x[2] * L_v(x[1], x[2]) - L(x[1], x[2]),\n        gradient = x -> [x[2] * L_qv(x[1], x[2]) - L_q(x[1], x[2]), x[2] * L_vv(x[1], x[2])],\n        rollout = variational_rollout(DiscreteLagrangian(nn), [q₀], [q₁], N))\nend\n\nmodel_del = discrete_model(nn_del)\nplot_model(\"DiscreteLagrangianNetwork\", model_del.acceleration, model_del.energy, model_del.rollout)\nFigure()","title":"The Discrete Lagrangian Network"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/lagrangian_neural_network/#Comparison","page":"Tutorial","text":"We close with three numbers per model, each independent of the gauge of the learned Lagrangian:\n\nacceleration_error, the relative error ddotq_NN - ddotq  ddotq on the grid [4];\nenergy_alignment, the mean of sintheta between the gradients of the learned and the true energy on the grid, 0 where the level sets agree [5];\ntrajectory_error, the largest distance between the rollout and the exact solution.\n\nFor the continuous networks the package computes all three. For the discrete network we compute the acceleration error from the acceleration above, as the same relative norm, and pass the gradient of its energy to energy_alignment as a function:\n\ncontinuous_row(name, nn, model) = (model = name,\n    acceleration_error = acceleration_error(nn, grid, Q̈_grid),\n    energy_alignment = energy_alignment(energy(nn), ∇E_true, grid),\n    trajectory_error = trajectory_error(model.rollout, Q_exact))\n\nresults = [\n    continuous_row(\"LNNLoss\", nn_lnn, model_lnn),\n    continuous_row(\"EulerLagrangeLoss\", nn_el, model_el),\n    continuous_row(\"VariationalMidpointLoss\", nn_vm, model_vm),\n    (model = \"DiscreteLagrangianNetwork\",\n        acceleration_error = sqrt(sum(abs2, model_del.acceleration(grid) - Q̈_grid) /\n                                  sum(abs2, Q̈_grid)),\n        energy_alignment = energy_alignment(model_del.gradient, ∇E_true, grid),\n        trajectory_error = trajectory_error(model_del.rollout, Q_exact))\n]\n\nprintln(rpad(\"model\", 28), rpad(\"acceleration_error\", 20), rpad(\"energy_alignment\", 18),\n    \"trajectory_error\")\nfor r in results\n    println(rpad(r.model, 28), rpad(round(r.acceleration_error; sigdigits = 3), 20),\n        rpad(round(r.energy_alignment; sigdigits = 3), 18), round(r.trajectory_error; sigdigits = 3))\nend\nmodel                       acceleration_error  energy_alignment  trajectory_error\nLNNLoss                     0.0105              0.00563           0.0146\nEulerLagrangeLoss           0.0158              0.00686           0.0152\nVariationalMidpointLoss     0.0858              0.0272            0.0168\nDiscreteLagrangianNetwork   0.0364              0.0132            0.0209\n\nThe networks trained on accelerations see the quantity the first measure compares, on the same square. The networks trained on positions see the dynamics only through one timestep, and what they learn is the Lagrangian whose discrete flow reproduces the data, which differs from the true one at order Deltat^2; modified_lagrangian corrects that offset. None of these numbers needs the learned Lagrangian to equal the true one.","title":"Comparison"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#Lagrangian-Neural-Network","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"A Lagrangian neural network learns a scalar Lagrangian L(q dotq) instead of the dynamics themselves. The dynamics are then whatever that Lagrangian implies, through the Euler–Lagrange equations\n\n    fracddtfracpartialLpartialdotq = fracpartialLpartialq\n\nThis is the Lagrangian counterpart of the Hamiltonian neural network of HamiltonianNeuralNetworks.jl: there the network is a Hamiltonian and the dynamics come from a Poisson tensor, here the network is a Lagrangian and the dynamics come from a variational principle.\n\nEach section below describes one method, cites its source, and documents its functions. The tutorial trains four models of the harmonic oscillator with them and compares the results.","title":"Lagrangian Neural Network"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#The-Architecture","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"LagrangianNeuralNetwork is the network of Cranmer et al. [1]: a fully connected network from the state x = q dotq to a scalar, which is read as the Lagrangian. Its default activation is softplus, which the source chose from a search over activations because a network of ReLUs has a zero second derivative, and the dynamics need the second. Hamzaogullari and Ozakin [2] find GeLU the best activation on their pendulum benchmarks; the package does not offer it yet.\n\nThe network is unstructured: any scalar function of the state is a candidate. The structured architecture L = frac12dotq^TM(q)dotq - V(q) of Lutter et al. [3], with the mass matrix M = hatLhatL^T from a Cholesky factor of positive diagonal, excludes singular velocity Hessians by construction; the package does not offer it yet.","title":"The Architecture"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#The-Loss","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"Expanding the Euler–Lagrange equations along a trajectory gives\n\n    nabla_dotqnabla_dotqLddotq\n        + (nabla_qnabla_dotqL)^Tdotq\n        = nabla_qL\n\nso the acceleration the learned Lagrangian predicts is\n\n    ddotq = (nabla_dotqnabla_dotqL)^-1\n               left( nabla_qL - (nabla_qnabla_dotqL)^Tdotq right)\n\nwhich is what LagrangianNeuralNetworks.lagrangian_acceleration computes and what LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LNNLoss compares against the data. This is the loss of Cranmer et al. [1].\n\nThe transpose is not cosmetic. nabla_qnabla_dotqL is indexed i j = partial^2Lpartialq_ipartialdotq_j, while the chain rule contracts the first index with dotq. It disappears only for a Lagrangian whose position–velocity coupling happens to be symmetric, which is why the test for this asserts a closed form whose coupling deliberately is not.","title":"The Loss"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#Why-the-Derivatives-Are-Symbolic","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"The loss needs the second derivative of the network with respect to its input, and then its own derivative with respect to the network parameters. Taking the inner one with Zygote inside the loss does not work: the parameter gradient then fails with MethodError: no method matching getindex(::IdDict{Any, Any}). The gradient and Hessian are therefore built as symbolic expressions with SymbolicNeuralNetworks and compiled, so that only one differentiation is left for Zygote to do. This is the same route that hamiltonian_vector_field of HamiltonianNeuralNetworks.jl takes, and for the same reason.","title":"Why the Derivatives Are Symbolic"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#The-Euler–Lagrange-Residual","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"EulerLagrangeLoss asks the learned Lagrangian to satisfy the Euler–Lagrange equations on the data, as the residual of the first equation above, with no solve against the velocity Hessian [4]. A velocity Hessian near zero then does not amplify a sample. The price is that the zero Lagrangian, and every null Lagrangian, has the loss zero: the loss is used together with a normalisation and a scale condition, as the next section describes.","title":"The Euler–Lagrange Residual"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#Normalisation","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"A Lagrangian is determined by its dynamics only up to a constant, a total time derivative and a factor (see the non-uniqueness of a Lagrangian). Offen [4] fixes the value and the momentum of the Lagrangian at one base point, Prop. 3.1, which removes the constant and the linear gauge and which NormalisedLagrangian does for every value of the parameters, and fixes the factor through the determinant of the velocity Hessian at that point, (B.1). ScaleCondition is that condition as a penalty, and DiscreteScaleCondition the condition (B.2) on the mixed Hessian of a discrete Lagrangian; Ober-Blöbaum and Offen [5] fix the scale of a discrete Lagrangian by a condition of this kind, (13)–(15).","title":"Normalisation"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#Regularity","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"A Lagrangian whose velocity Hessian is singular does not determine an acceleration, and a discrete Lagrangian whose mixed Hessian is singular does not determine the next position. Offen and Ober-Blöbaum [6] penalise the norm of the inverse of that Hessian, and Offen and Ober-Blöbaum [7] tame the penalty so that it vanishes wherever the inverse is small enough, (34); Lishkova et al. [8] penalise a small determinant through a sigmoid. VelocityHessianRegulariser is the penalty of a continuous Lagrangian, and MixedHessianRegulariser that of the midpoint discrete Lagrangian, in the three forms. RegularisedLoss adds weighted penalties, these or the scale conditions, to any loss of the package.","title":"Regularity"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#Training-on-Positions-Alone","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LNNLoss needs ddotq in the data. When only positions are available, LagrangianNeuralNetworks.VariationalMidpointLoss trains the same architecture through the discrete Euler–Lagrange equations of the midpoint discrete Lagrangian\n\n    L_d(q_n q_n+1) = Deltat\n        Lleft( fracq_n + q_n+12 fracq_n+1 - q_nDeltat right)\n\nwhich ask that D_2L_d(q_n q_n+1) + D_1L_d(q_n+1 q_n+2) = 0 along the data. The loss is the mean squared residual of these equations, (16) of Ober-Blöbaum and Offen [5]. The residual needs only the first derivative of the network, because the chain rule through the midpoint is written out rather than taken with a nested Zygote.gradient; its default regulariser, LagrangianNeuralNetworks.MixedHessianRegulariser, needs the second.","title":"Training on Positions Alone"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#The-Discrete-Lagrangian-Network","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"Lishkova et al. [8] learn the discrete Lagrangian L_d(q_0 q_1) itself, as a network of two consecutive positions, (14), with the squared discrete Euler–Lagrange residual as the loss. DiscreteLagrangianNetwork is that network and DiscreteEulerLagrangeLoss that loss. The continuous Lagrangian a discrete one stands for is its inverse modified Lagrangian, (4) of the source, which inverse_modified_lagrangian reads off the network.","title":"The Discrete Lagrangian Network"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#The-Variational-Integrator","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"A learned Lagrangian is rolled out by the variational integrator of its discrete Lagrangian: each step solves the discrete Euler–Lagrange equations for the next position by Newton's method [6]. DiscreteLagrangian is the discrete model, the midpoint rule of a continuous network or a discrete network itself, and variational_rollout the rollout.","title":"The Variational Integrator"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#Backward-Error-Analysis","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"A model trained on positions learns the Lagrangian whose midpoint rule reproduces the data, its inverse modified Lagrangian, which differs from the true Lagrangian of the data at O(Deltat^2). Ober-Blöbaum and Offen [5] give the map from the one to the other to second order in the timestep, (11), and Lishkova et al. [8] the inverse modified Lagrangian of a discrete network, (4). modified_lagrangian applies the map, and removes that offset.","title":"Backward Error Analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#The-Non-Uniqueness-of-a-Lagrangian","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"The data fix the equations of motion, not the Lagrangian. If L gives the dynamics, so does\n\n    tildeL = rhoL + c + fracddtF(q)\n\nfor every scale factor rho neq 0, every constant c and every total time derivative of a function F of the positions, and in one dimension even Lagrangians that are not of this form, inequivalent ones, give the same equations of motion. A learned Lagrangian is therefore not compared with the true one: a fit of the one to the other can be poor where the dynamics are learned well. The normalisation of Offen [4] removes the constant, the linear gauge and the scale, but a model without it is one representative of a whole class.\n\nThe package measures a learned model by what does not depend on the representative:\n\nacceleration_error, the relative error of the acceleration that the model predicts on a grid of states, §7.3 of [4];\nthe energy E = dotq^Tpartial_dotqL - L, which becomes rho E - c under the gauge, so that its level sets do not change, and energy_alignment, the mean sintheta between the gradients of the learned and the true energy, the measure nu of §3.1 of [5];\ntrajectory_error, the largest distance between a rollout of the model and a reference trajectory.","title":"The Non-Uniqueness of a Lagrangian"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LagrangianArchitecture","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"LagrangianArchitecture <: Architecture\n\nThe supertype of an architecture whose network is a scalar Lagrangian L(q dotq) of an input that stacks q on dotq.\n\nThe losses of a continuous Lagrangian take any LagrangianArchitecture; the discrete loss takes a DiscreteLagrangianArchitecture. A subtype needs two methods: AbstractNeuralNetworks.dim(arch), the input dimension 2n, and Chain(arch), a chain with a scalar output. Without a dim method, AbstractNeuralNetworks logs an error and returns nothing, and the constructor of a loss fails there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LagrangianArchitecture"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LagrangianNeuralNetwork","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"LagrangianNeuralNetwork(dimin; width = dimin, nhidden = 1, activation = softplus)\n\nA Lagrangian L(q dotq) as a network of dimin = 2n inputs and a scalar output: a Dense layer of width neurons, nhidden more of them, and a bias-free Linear output layer. The hidden layers apply activation, by default softplus.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LagrangianNeuralNetwork"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.softplus","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"softplus(x)\n\nThe activation log(1 + e^x), with the element type of x, evaluated in the split form x + log(1 + e^-x) for x  0 and log(1 + e^x) otherwise. Neither branch overflows: the positive one adds a term that decays to 0, the negative one an exponent of at most 1. For a large positive x the value is x to the accuracy of x, and for a large negative one e^x to the accuracy of exp. So softplus(-Inf) is 0, softplus(Inf) is Inf, and softplus(0) is log 2.\n\nIt is the default activation of LagrangianNeuralNetwork. With tanh, LNNLoss does not learn the harmonic oscillator: the solve against nabla_dotqnabla_dotqL amplifies every sample where that block is near zero. With softplus it does; scripts/reproduction/k2_harmonic_oscillator.jl trains it on 16 seeds in each precision.\n\nsoftplus is a symbolic primitive, so the compiled first and second derivatives of a network are the logistic sigma and its derivative sigma(1 - sigma), both bounded and finite in Float32 and in Float64: the symbolic derivative of softplus is registered as the logistic, and the split is never differentiated symbolically, so its tie-break at 0 changes no derivative.\n\nLogExpFunctions also exports a softplus; a module that loads both packages qualifies the name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.softplus"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LNNLoss","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"LNNLoss <: NetworkLoss\n\nThe loss for a Lagrangian neural network.\n\nThe network output is a scalar Lagrangian L(q dotq). The loss compares the acceleration its Euler–Lagrange equations predict against the acceleration in the data:\n\n    mathttloss(mathcalNN mathttinput mathttoutput)\n        = ddotq(mathttinput) - mathttoutput  mathttoutput\n\nwhere mathttinput stacks q on dotq and is therefore 2n rows tall, and mathttoutput is ddotq and is n rows tall. See lagrangian_acceleration for ddotq.\n\nConstructor\n\nThis can be called with a LagrangianArchitecture as its only argument:\n\nLNNLoss(arch)\n\nFunctor\n\nloss(model, ps, input, output)\nloss(ps, input, output) # equivalent to the above\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LNNLoss"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.lagrangian_acceleration","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"lagrangian_acceleration(arch::LagrangianArchitecture)\n\nCompute an executable expression of the acceleration that the Lagrangian arch predicts.\n\nThe Euler–Lagrange equations of a Lagrangian L(q dotq) are\n\n    fracddtfracpartialLpartialdotq = fracpartialLpartialq\n\nwhich, expanded along a trajectory, reads\n\n    nabla_dotqnabla_dotqLddotq\n        + (nabla_qnabla_dotqL)^Tdotq\n        = nabla_qL\n\nso the acceleration is\n\n    ddotq = (nabla_dotqnabla_dotqL)^-1\n               left( nabla_qL - (nabla_qnabla_dotqL)^Tdotq right)\n\nThe transpose is not cosmetic. nabla_qnabla_dotqL is indexed i j = partial^2Lpartialq_ipartialdotq_j, while the chain rule contracts the first index with dotq. It disappears only for a Lagrangian whose position–velocity coupling happens to be symmetric.\n\nImplementation\n\nThe gradient and the Hessian of the network with respect to its input come from LagrangianDerivatives, compiled once when lagrangian_acceleration is called. Trailing input dimensions beyond the first are flattened on the way in and restored on the way out, so an input of size (2n s B), which is what Batch hands the loss, yields an acceleration of size (n s B).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.lagrangian_acceleration"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks._euler_lagrange_acceleration","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"_euler_lagrange_acceleration(H, g, q̇)\n\nSolve the Euler–Lagrange equations of one sample for the acceleration.\n\nH is the full 2ntimes2n Hessian of the Lagrangian with respect to its input, g the full 2n gradient, and q̇ the n velocity components of that same input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks._euler_lagrange_acceleration"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LagrangianDerivatives","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"LagrangianDerivatives(arch::Architecture; hessian = true)\n\nCompile the gradient and the Hessian of the network of arch with respect to its input, once.\n\narch is any architecture whose Chain has a scalar output and that has a method of AbstractNeuralNetworks.dim, the input dimension 2n; it need not be a LagrangianArchitecture. Evaluate the result with input_gradient and input_hessian. With hessian = false, only the gradient is built and compiled, and input_hessian throws an ArgumentError.\n\nImplementation\n\nThe gradient and the Hessian are built as symbolic expressions with SymbolicNeuralNetworks.Jacobian, applied once and then again to its own result, and compiled with build_nn_function. They are not taken with Zygote inside a loss: differentiating a loss that itself contains a Zygote.gradient with respect to the network parameters fails with MethodError: no method matching getindex(::IdDict{Any, Any}). This is the route hamiltonian_vector_field of HamiltonianNeuralNetworks.jl takes, and for the same reason.\n\nThe compiled functions flatten a batch along the columns, (1 2ncdotB) for the gradient and (2n 2ncdotB) for the Hessian, so input_gradient and input_hessian reshape their output.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LagrangianDerivatives"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.input_gradient","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"input_gradient(D::LagrangianDerivatives, x, ps)\n\nThe gradient of the network with respect to its input, nabla_xL, at each column of x, for the parameters ps.\n\nFor x of size (2n B) the result has size (2n B). Trailing dimensions are flattened on the way in and restored on the way out: x of size (2n s B) gives (2n s B). An x whose first dimension is not 2n throws a DimensionMismatch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.input_gradient"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.input_hessian","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"input_hessian(D::LagrangianDerivatives, x, ps)\n\nThe Hessian of the network with respect to its input, nabla_xnabla_xL, at each column of x, for the parameters ps.\n\nFor x of size (2n B) the result has size (2n 2n B), and slice b is the Hessian at column b. Trailing dimensions are flattened on the way in and restored on the way out: x of size (2n s B) gives (2n 2n s B). An x whose first dimension is not 2n throws a DimensionMismatch, and D built with hessian = false an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.input_hessian"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.EulerLagrangeLoss","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"EulerLagrangeLoss <: NetworkLoss\n\nThe Euler–Lagrange residual loss for a Lagrangian neural network (Offen 2025, App. B).\n\nThe loss asks the learned Lagrangian to satisfy the Euler–Lagrange equations on the data, with no solve for the acceleration:\n\n    mathttloss(mathcalNN mathttinput mathttoutput)\n        = frac1Bsum_b=1^B left\n            nabla_dotqnabla_dotqLddotq_b\n            + (nabla_qnabla_dotqL)^Tdotq_b\n            - nabla_qL right^2\n\nwith the derivatives of L taken at sample b and (nabla_qnabla_dotqL)_ij = partial^2Lpartialq_ipartialdotq_j. input stacks q on dotq and has 2n rows, and output is ddotq and has n; trailing dimensions are samples too, so an input of size (2n s B) goes with an output of size (n s B), and the mean runs over all sB samples. See _euler_lagrange_residual for the residual of one sample.\n\nUnlike LNNLoss, the loss inverts no Hessian, so a velocity Hessian near zero does not amplify a sample. It is not normalised: the zero Lagrangian has the loss zero on any data.\n\nA size mismatch of input and output throws a DimensionMismatch, and an empty batch an ArgumentError. NaN and Inf in the data propagate to the value.\n\nConstructor\n\nEulerLagrangeLoss(arch)\n\nwhere arch is a LagrangianArchitecture. An odd input dimension dim(arch) throws an ArgumentError, before anything is compiled.\n\nFunctor\n\nloss(model, ps, input, output)\nloss(ps, input, output) # equivalent to the above\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.EulerLagrangeLoss"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks._euler_lagrange_residual","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"_euler_lagrange_residual(H, g, q̇, q̈)\n\nThe Euler–Lagrange residual of one sample (Offen 2025, App. B),\n\n    nabla_dotqnabla_dotqLddotq + (nabla_qnabla_dotqL)^Tdotq - nabla_qL\n\nwhere H is the 2ntimes2n Hessian of the Lagrangian with respect to its input (q dotq), g the 2n gradient, and q̇ and q̈ the n velocities and accelerations of the sample. The transpose contracts the first index of (nabla_qnabla_dotqL)_ij = partial^2Lpartialq_ipartialdotq_j with dotq, as in _euler_lagrange_acceleration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks._euler_lagrange_residual"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.NormalisedLagrangian","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"NormalisedLagrangian(arch::LagrangianArchitecture; base = zeros(2n), value = 0, momentum = zeros(n))\n\nThe Lagrangian of arch with its value and its momentum fixed at one base point (Offen 2025, Prop. 3.1), where 2n is dim(arch):\n\n    tildeL(x) = L(x) - L(x_b) - (dotq - dotq_b)^Tleft(nabla_dotqL(x_b) - p_bright) + c_b\n\nwith x = (q dotq), L the network of arch, x_b = base, dotq_b its last n entries, c_b = value and p_b = momentum. Then tildeL(x_b) = c_b and nabla_dotqtildeL(x_b) = p_b for every value of the parameters, which removes the constant and the linear gauge of a Lagrangian. With value and momentum both zero, the scale is not fixed, and is left to a condition of its own.\n\nThe network takes the same input as that of arch and has the same parameters, nested under the key L1. Every loss of a continuous Lagrangian takes a NormalisedLagrangian as it takes arch.\n\nnabla_dotqL(x_b) is computed in the forward pass, by a reverse pass over the layers of Chain(arch), so it is part of the expression that SymbolicNeuralNetworks traces and it depends on the parameters. That reverse pass supports Dense and Linear layers with the activations tanh, softplus and identity.\n\nbase and momentum are copied at construction. base, value and momentum are converted to the element type of the parameters. Where the network of arch is not finite at the base point, the network of the wrapper is not finite anywhere.\n\nA base or a momentum that is no AbstractVector throws a TypeError. A base of a length other than 2n or a momentum of a length other than n throws a DimensionMismatch. An odd dim(arch), a layer of Chain(arch) that is no Dense, and an activation other than the three above throw an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.NormalisedLagrangian"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.ScaleCondition","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"ScaleCondition(arch; base = zeros(2n), value = 1)\n\nThe scale normalisation of a continuous Lagrangian (Offen 2025, (B.1)):\n\n    left( detnabla_dotqnabla_dotqL(x_b) - c right)^2\n\nat the point x_b = base = q_b dotq_b, with c = value. It fixes the factor by which a Lagrangian is determined. arch is a LagrangianArchitecture of 2n inputs.\n\nThe result r is called as r(ps, input); the value does not depend on input, whose row count must still be 2n. base and value are converted to the element type of input, which the value has. A value that is not positive and finite throws an ArgumentError, and a base of another length than 2n or an input of another row count a DimensionMismatch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.ScaleCondition"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.DiscreteScaleCondition","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"DiscreteScaleCondition(arch, Δt; base = zeros(2n), value = 1)\n\nThe scale normalisation of the midpoint discrete Lagrangian of a continuous Lagrangian (Offen 2025, (B.2)):\n\n    left( detLambda(x_b) - c right)^2 qquad\n    Lambda = -Deltatfracpartial^2L_dpartialq_0partialq_1\n\nwith x_b = base the pair q_0 q_1, Lambda at its midpoint (see _mixed_hessian), and c = value. The source has detpartial^2L_dpartialq_0partialq_1; the package uses detLambda = (-Deltat)^ndetpartial^2L_dpartialq_0partialq_1, which tends to the continuous detnabla_dotqnabla_dotqL of ScaleCondition as Deltat to 0.\n\nThe result r is called as r(ps, input), as that of ScaleCondition is, with the same conversions and refusals; a timestep that is not positive and finite throws an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.DiscreteScaleCondition"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.RegularisedLoss","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"RegularisedLoss(loss, terms...)\n\nThe loss loss, a NetworkLoss, plus weighted penalties: each term is a pair weight => r, and the value is\n\n    mathttloss(ps mathttinput mathttoutput) + sum_k w_kr_k(ps mathttinput)\n\nA penalty r is any callable r(ps, input), such as VelocityHessianRegulariser, MixedHessianRegulariser, ScaleCondition or DiscreteScaleCondition. Each weight is converted to the element type of input; with no terms, the value is that of loss. A weight that is negative, NaN or Inf throws an ArgumentError. A weight of 0 is allowed, and its penalty is not evaluated, so a penalty of Inf or NaN adds nothing there. RegularisedLoss checks no row count itself: it evaluates loss before its penalties, so data that loss refuses throw the error of loss.\n\nFunctor\n\nloss(model, ps, input, output)\nloss(ps, input, output) # equivalent to the above\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.RegularisedLoss"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.VelocityHessianRegulariser","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"VelocityHessianRegulariser(arch)\n\nThe regularity penalty of a continuous Lagrangian, the continuous counterpart of the penalty (11) of Offen & Ober-Blöbaum 2023 (see MixedHessianRegulariser, whose Lambda tends to the velocity Hessian as Deltat to 0): the mean over the samples of\n\n    left (nabla_dotqnabla_dotqL)^-1 right_F^2 \n\nwhich grows where the velocity Hessian is near singular, as it is for a null or a degenerate Lagrangian. arch is a LagrangianArchitecture.\n\nAgainst the continuous penalty sum(partial^2Lpartialdotq^2)^-1, the norm is here squared, taken in the Frobenius norm in place of the spectral norm (the two are equal for n = 1, and the Frobenius norm is an upper bound otherwise), and averaged over the samples.\n\nThe result r is called as r(ps, input), with input the points q dotq: 2n rows, and any trailing dimensions, which count as samples. The value has the element type of input. A singular velocity Hessian gives Inf, and an input of another row count than 2n throws a DimensionMismatch. Add it to a loss with RegularisedLoss.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.VelocityHessianRegulariser"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.MixedHessianRegulariser","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"MixedHessianRegulariser(arch, Δt; form = :tamed)\n\nThe regularity penalty of the midpoint discrete Lagrangian L_d(q_0 q_1) = DeltatL((q_0 + q_1)2 (q_1 - q_0)Deltat) of a continuous Lagrangian: the mean over the pairs q_0 q_1 of a penalty of\n\n    Lambda = -Deltatfracpartial^2L_dpartialq_0partialq_1\n\nsee _mixed_hessian. Lambda tends to the velocity Hessian nabla_dotqnabla_dotqL as Deltat to 0, so a threshold on it means the same as one on the velocity Hessian. arch is a LagrangianArchitecture, and Δt the timestep.\n\nThe three forms:\n\n:inverse: Lambda^-1_F^2 (Offen & Ober-Blöbaum 2023, (11)). The source, a paper on PDEs, penalises (D_1D_2L_d)^-1^2 in the spectral norm; with Lambda = -DeltatD_1D_2L_d, this is that penalty divided by Deltat^2, and in the Frobenius norm.\n:tamed: mathrmrelu(1 - 10Lambda^-1_F^2) (Offen & Ober-Blöbaum 2024, (34)), which is 0 wherever Lambda^-1_F^2 le 10; the threshold 10 applies to the Frobenius norm of Lambda^-1.\n:sigmoid: 1 - sigma(001det(Lambda)^2) with sigma(x) = 1(1 + e^-x) (Lishkova 2023, the degeneracy term added to the cost function (14), with m = 2). The source uses d = det D_2D_1L_d; here detLambda = (-Deltat)^ndet D_1D_2L_d, so that the three forms read the same matrix, and the square removes the sign.\n\nA singular Lambda gives Inf for :inverse, 1 for :tamed and 1/2 for :sigmoid.\n\nThe result r is called as r(ps, input), with input the pairs q_0 q_1: 2n rows, and any trailing dimensions, which count as samples. The value has the element type of input, and Δt is converted to it. A timestep that is not positive and finite and an unknown form throw an ArgumentError, and an input of another row count than 2n a DimensionMismatch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.MixedHessianRegulariser"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks._mixed_hessian","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"_mixed_hessian(H, Δt)\n\nThe matrix Lambda = -DeltatD_1D_2L_d of the midpoint discrete Lagrangian L_d(a b) = DeltatL((a + b)2 (b - a)Deltat), from the 2ntimes2n Hessian H of L at the midpoint, with D_1D_2L_di j = partial^2L_dpartiala_ipartialb_j.\n\nBy the chain rule, with H in the blocks H_qq, H_qdotq and H_dotqdotq, H_qdotqi j = partial^2Lpartialq_ipartialdotq_j,\n\n    Lambda = H_dotqdotq\n        - fracDeltat2left(H_qdotq - H_qdotq^Tright)\n        - fracDeltat^24H_qq\n\nwhich tends to the velocity Hessian H_dotqdotq as Deltat to 0. Δt is converted to the element type of H.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks._mixed_hessian"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks._regularity_penalty","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"_regularity_penalty(Λ, form)\n\nThe penalty of one sample of MixedHessianRegulariser on the matrix Λ, for form :inverse, :tamed or :sigmoid, in the element type of Λ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks._regularity_penalty"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks._check_form","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"_check_form(form)\n\nReturn form if it is one of the forms of MixedHessianRegulariser, :inverse, :tamed or :sigmoid, and throw an ArgumentError otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks._check_form"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.VariationalMidpointLoss","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"VariationalMidpointLoss <: NetworkLoss\n\nThe loss that trains a Lagrangian neural network on positions alone, through the discrete Euler–Lagrange equations of the midpoint discrete Lagrangian.\n\nIt needs three consecutive positions and the timestep between them — no velocities and no accelerations.\n\nThe midpoint discrete Lagrangian is\n\n    L_d(q_n q_n+1) = Deltat\n        Lleft( fracq_n + q_n+12 fracq_n+1 - q_nDeltat right)\n\nand the discrete Euler–Lagrange equations ask that\n\n    D_2L_d(q_n q_n+1) + D_1L_d(q_n+1 q_n+2) = 0 \n\nThe loss is the mean over the samples of the squared residual (Ober-Blöbaum & Offen 2023, (16)), plus a weighted regularity penalty:\n\n    frac1Bsum_b=1^B left D_2L_d(q_n q_n+1) + D_1L_d(q_n+1 q_n+2) right^2\n        + wr(ps q_n q_n+1)\n\nThe residual alone does not exclude a null Lagrangian: a constant, or g(q)cdotdotq with g = nablavarphi and varphi quadratic, has a discrete residual of zero on any data. The default penalty, MixedHessianRegulariser in the form :tamed, is 1 where the discrete Lagrangian is degenerate, as it is for those. The relative normalisation of the source is not applied.\n\nImplementation\n\nThe two slot derivatives are written out with the chain rule rather than taken with a nested Zygote.gradient:\n\n    D_1L_d = fracDeltat2nabla_qL - nabla_dotqL qquad\n    D_2L_d = fracDeltat2nabla_qL + nabla_dotqL\n\nboth evaluated at the midpoint. The residual needs only the first derivative of the network with respect to its input, and that comes from the compiled symbolic expression of LagrangianDerivatives, exactly as that of hamiltonian_vector_field of HamiltonianNeuralNetworks.jl does; the regulariser compiles its own derivatives. Written the other way — a Zygote.gradient inside the loss — the parameter gradient fails with MethodError: no method matching getindex(::IdDict{Any, Any}).\n\nConstructor\n\nVariationalMidpointLoss(arch, timestep;\n    regulariser = MixedHessianRegulariser(arch, timestep; form = :tamed), weight = 1)\n\nwhere arch is a LagrangianArchitecture, regulariser a callable r(ps, input) and weight its weight w. A timestep that is not positive and finite, and a weight that is negative, NaN or Inf, throw an ArgumentError. With weight = 0 the regulariser is not evaluated, and the loss is the mean squared residual alone.\n\nFunctor\n\nloss(model, ps, input, output)\nloss(ps, input, output) # equivalent to the above\n\ninput stacks q_n on q_n+1 and so has 2n rows; output is q_n+2 and has n. Trailing dimensions are samples. Another row count throws a DimensionMismatch. The timestep and the weight are converted to the element type of the data, which the value has.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.VariationalMidpointLoss"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks._discrete_lagrangian_derivatives","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"_discrete_lagrangian_derivatives(loss, ps, qa, qb)\n\nReturn (D₁Lᵈ, D₂Lᵈ) of the midpoint discrete Lagrangian on the pair (qa, qb), one column per sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks._discrete_lagrangian_derivatives"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.DiscreteLagrangianArchitecture","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"DiscreteLagrangianArchitecture <: Architecture\n\nThe supertype of an architecture whose network is a discrete Lagrangian L_d(q_0 q_1), a scalar function of two consecutive positions (Lishkova 2023, (14)).\n\nIt is no LagrangianArchitecture: its input [q_0; q_1] stacks two positions, not a position and a velocity, so a loss that reads a position and a velocity, such as LNNLoss, would take the second position for a velocity. A subtype needs two methods, AbstractNeuralNetworks.dim(arch), the input dimension 2n, and Chain(arch), a chain of 2n inputs with a scalar output. The subtype of the package is DiscreteLagrangianNetwork, which DiscreteEulerLagrangeLoss trains and from which inverse_modified_lagrangian reads a continuous Lagrangian.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.DiscreteLagrangianArchitecture"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.DiscreteLagrangianNetwork","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"DiscreteLagrangianNetwork(n; width = 2n, nhidden = 1, activation = softplus)\n\nThe discrete Lagrangian L_d(q_0 q_1) of Lishkova 2023, (14), as a network: a Dense layer of width neurons, nhidden more of them, and a bias-free Linear output layer. Its input [q_0; q_1] stacks two positions of n entries, and its output is a scalar.\n\nThe network is a DiscreteLagrangianArchitecture, not a LagrangianArchitecture. DiscreteEulerLagrangeLoss trains it on positions alone, through the discrete Euler–Lagrange equations, and inverse_modified_lagrangian reads a continuous Lagrangian off it. The layers are those of LagrangianNeuralNetwork, with 2n inputs in place of dimin, and the default activation is softplus.\n\nn, width and nhidden are stored as Int. n < 1, width < 1 and nhidden < 0 throw an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.DiscreteLagrangianNetwork"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.DiscreteEulerLagrangeLoss","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"DiscreteEulerLagrangeLoss(arch, Δt;\n    regulariser = MixedHessianRegulariser(arch, Δt; form = :tamed), weight = 1)\n\nThe loss that trains a DiscreteLagrangianNetwork on positions alone, through the discrete Euler–Lagrange equations of the network itself:\n\n    D_2L_d(q_k-1 q_k) + D_1L_d(q_k q_k+1) = 0 \n\nIt is the mean over the samples of the squared residual (Lishkova 2023, (14)), plus a weighted regularity penalty:\n\n    frac1Bsum_b=1^B left D_2L_d(q_k-1 q_k) + D_1L_d(q_k q_k+1) right^2\n        + wr(ps q_k-1 q_k)\n\nwith D_1L_d and D_2L_d the first and the last n entries of the input gradient of the network, and w = weight. The residual alone does not exclude a null discrete Lagrangian: a constant, and g(q_0) - g(q_1) with g linear, have a residual of zero on any data. The default penalty, MixedHessianRegulariser in the form :tamed, is 1 where the discrete Lagrangian is degenerate, as it is for those; its Lambda = -DeltatD_1D_2L_d tends to the velocity Hessian of the continuous Lagrangian as Deltat to 0.\n\nThe residual needs only the first derivative of the network with respect to its input, which comes from the compiled symbolic expression of LagrangianDerivatives, and the regulariser compiles its own Hessian.\n\nConstructor\n\narch is a DiscreteLagrangianArchitecture, Δt its timestep, regulariser a callable r(ps, input) and weight its weight w. The timestep is held by the regulariser, which the constructor builds from it; the loss itself refuses a timestep that is not positive and finite, and a weight that is negative, NaN or Inf, with an ArgumentError. With weight = 0 the regulariser is not evaluated, and the loss is the mean squared residual alone.\n\nFunctor\n\nloss(model, ps, input, output)\nloss(ps, input, output) # equivalent to the above\n\ninput stacks q_k-1 on q_k and so has 2n rows; output is q_k+1 and has n. Trailing dimensions are samples. Another row count throws a DimensionMismatch. The value has the element type of the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.DiscreteEulerLagrangeLoss"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.inverse_modified_lagrangian","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"inverse_modified_lagrangian(nn::NeuralNetwork, Δt)\ninverse_modified_lagrangian(Ld::Function, Δt)\n\nThe continuous Lagrangian that a discrete Lagrangian stands for, the inverse modified Lagrangian of Lishkova 2023, (4):\n\n    L(q dotq) = frac1Deltat\n        L_dleft(q - fracDeltat2dotq q + fracDeltat2dotqright)\n\nthe formula of the source divided by Deltat. The division is deliberate: it makes the result approximate L itself, and for the midpoint discrete Lagrangian L_d of a continuous L it is that L exactly.\n\nnn is a NeuralNetwork of a DiscreteLagrangianNetwork; with a function in its place, Ld(q_0, q_1) is called directly, which is how the identity above is checked against an analytic discrete Lagrangian.\n\nThe result f is called as f(q, q̇) on a pair of vectors q and q̇ or on a pair of n × B matrices, and on no other shape: a scalar for the vectors and a 1 × B array for the matrices, in the element type of q, which Deltat is converted to. A plain Ld is called on one pair of positions at a time, as two AbstractVectors — the columns of the pair on a matrix call — and must return a number or a one-element array. A q̇ of another size than q throws a DimensionMismatch, and so does a q of another row count than n with the network method; a timestep that is not positive and finite throws an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.inverse_modified_lagrangian"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.DiscreteLagrangian","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"DiscreteLagrangian(nn, Δt)\nDiscreteLagrangian(nn)\nDiscreteLagrangian(D₁L_d, D₂L_d, D₁D₂L_d)\n\nThe discrete Lagrangian L_d(a b) of two consecutive positions, the model that variational_rollout integrates, in its three forms.\n\nDiscreteLagrangian(nn, Δt) is the midpoint discrete Lagrangian of a NeuralNetwork of a LagrangianArchitecture,\n\n    L_d(a b) = DeltatLleft(fraca + b2 fracb - aDeltatright)\n\nread through the chain rule as the two slot derivatives and the mixed Hessian\n\n    D_1L_d = fracDeltat2nabla_qL - nabla_dotqL qquad\n    D_2L_d = fracDeltat2nabla_qL + nabla_dotqL qquad\n    D_1D_2L_d = fracDeltat4H_qq + frac12left(H_qdotq - H_dotqqright)\n        - frac1DeltatH_dotqdotq\n        = -fracLambdaDeltat\n\nall at the midpoint, with Lambda the -DeltatD_1D_2L_d that MixedHessianRegulariser builds from the same input Hessian. The timestep is converted to the element type of the network's parameters.\n\nDiscreteLagrangian(nn) is a NeuralNetwork of a DiscreteLagrangianArchitecture, which is a discrete Lagrangian already: its input [a; b] gives D_1L_d = g1n, D_2L_d = gn+12n and D_1D_2L_d = H1n n+12n as blocks of its own input gradient g and input Hessian H.\n\nDiscreteLagrangian(D₁L_d, D₂L_d, D₁D₂L_d) takes three functions of (a, b) — the two slot derivatives as vectors of n entries and the mixed Hessian as an ntimesn matrix, indexed i j = partial^2L_dpartiala_ipartialb_j. It names no precision and no dimension of its own: a rollout takes both from the positions.\n\nThe slot derivatives and the momentum map p = -D_1L_d are those of Offen 2023, Prop. 1, and Ober-Blöbaum & Offen 2023, §2.3. A timestep that is not positive and finite in the parameters' element type throws an ArgumentError, and so does a bare continuous network, whose message names DiscreteLagrangian(nn, Δt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.DiscreteLagrangian"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.variational_rollout","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"variational_rollout(model, q₀, q₁, N; momentum = false, tol, maxiter = 20)\nvariational_rollout(model, q₀, p₀, N; momentum = true, tol, maxiter = 20)\n\nRoll a trajectory out of a discrete Lagrangian model: the n × (N + 1) matrix [q₀ q₁ … q_N] of the positions q₀ and q₁ and the N − 1 that follow them.\n\nEach step solves the discrete Euler–Lagrange equations (Offen 2023, Prop. 1)\n\n    D_2L_d(q_k-1 q_k) + D_1L_d(q_k q_k+1) = 0\n\nfor q_k+1 by Newton's method, with D_1D_2L_d(q_k cdot) as the Jacobian and 2q_k - q_k-1 as the guess, until the 2-norm of the residual is at most tol. The positions are numbered from zero, so the message of a failed solve names the position it was solving for as q_k with that index.\n\nWith momentum = true the second argument is the momentum p_0 = -D_1L_d(q_0 q_1) rather than a position: q₁ is recovered first, by solving -D_1L_d(q_0 q_1) = p_0 from the guess q₀, and the rollout then proceeds from the pair. Both forms take four positional arguments of the same types, which is why the keyword selects this one. N = 0 returns q₀ alone, as one column, and solves nothing.\n\ntol defaults to sqrtepsilon(T), absolute and in the units of the residual, which scale with the Lagrangian. maxiter counts Newton steps, and the residual is tested before the first step and after each one; a residual that is not finite stops the solve at once, and one that does not reach tol in maxiter steps throws an ErrorException naming the position.\n\nThe element type T of the result is the parameters' for a network model and float(promote_type(eltype(q₀), eltype(q₁))) for a model of functions, and the positions are converted to it, as is the momentum with momentum = true. model is a DiscreteLagrangian or a NeuralNetwork of a DiscreteLagrangianArchitecture; a continuous network throws an ArgumentError naming DiscreteLagrangian(nn, Δt).\n\nN < 0, a tol that is not positive and a maxiter below one throw an ArgumentError; positions of another length than each other or than the network's throw a DimensionMismatch; a singular Jacobian throws a LinearAlgebra.SingularException.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.variational_rollout"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.modified_lagrangian","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"modified_lagrangian(model, Δt)\n\nThe continuous Lagrangian whose exact flow the discrete model of model reproduces to O(Deltat^4), as the function (q, q̇) ↦ L + (Δt²/24)·[(L_q − L_qq̇·q̇)²/L_q̇q̇ − L_qq·q̇²] of two vectors of one entry.\n\nThis is the second-order backward error analysis of Ober-Blöbaum & Offen 2023, (11), in the direction that recovers the true Lagrangian. The midpoint rule reproduces the exact flow of its inverse modified Lagrangian, which for a continuous network is the network itself and for a DiscreteLagrangianNetwork is L_im(q dotq) = L_d(q - Deltatdotq2 q + Deltatdotq2)Deltat (Lishkova 2023, (4)). The formula maps that inverse modified Lagrangian to the true one; its plus sign gives a frequency error of O(Deltat^4) on the harmonic oscillator and the opposite sign O(Deltat^2).\n\nFor a continuous network the derivatives are the compiled input derivatives of the network. For a DiscreteLagrangianArchitecture they are the chain rule through the midpoint, with H the blocks of the network's own input Hessian at [q − Δt q̇/2; q + Δt q̇/2]:\n\n    L_q = fracD_1L_d + D_2L_dDeltat qquad\n    L_dotq = fracD_2L_d - D_1L_d2 qquad\n    L_qq = fracH_11 + H_12 + H_21 + H_22Deltat\n\n    L_qdotq = fracH_12 + H_22 - H_11 - H_212 qquad\n    L_dotqdotq = fracDeltat4left(H_11 - H_12 - H_21 + H_22right)\n\nThe analysis is one-dimensional: a model of more than one degree of freedom throws an ArgumentError, and so does a timestep that is not positive and finite in the element type of the parameters. A q or a q̇ of another length throws a DimensionMismatch. A zero L_dotqdotq gives the IEEE value of the quotient, and no exception. The value has the element type of the network's parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.modified_lagrangian"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LagrangianEnergy","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"LagrangianEnergy\n\nThe energy of a continuous Lagrangian and its gradient, as energy returns them. The object is called as E(q, q̇) for the value, and its field gradient as gradient(x) for the gradient of the energy at the state x = [q; q̇].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LagrangianEnergy"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.energy","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"energy(model)\n\nThe energy of the continuous Lagrangian of model, a callable E(q, q̇) = q̇ᵀ∂_q̇L − L of two vectors of n entries, with the element type of the network's parameters.\n\nIt is the quantity a trajectory conserves when the Lagrangian does not depend on time, and it is the measure of a learned model whose level sets are compared with those of a reference (Offen 2025, §7.3). A Lagrangian is unique only up to a scale, a constant and a total time derivative, and the energy changes with exactly the first two: under L mapsto rho L + c + fracddtF(q) it becomes rho E - c. Its value therefore carries the gauge, and its gradient carries only the factor rho, so its level sets are unchanged.\n\nThe field gradient of the result is the gradient of the energy with respect to the state x = q dotq,\n\n    nabla E = Hbeginpmatrix0 dotqendpmatrix\n        - beginpmatrixnabla_qL 0endpmatrix\n\nfrom the compiled input gradient nabla_qL and the input Hessian H of the network, which is what energy_alignment compares. It takes a vector of 2n entries.\n\nenergy needs a continuous Lagrangian, and a DiscreteLagrangianArchitecture throws an ArgumentError. A q or a q̇ of another length throws a DimensionMismatch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.energy"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.acceleration_error","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"acceleration_error(model, x, q̈_ref)\n\nThe relative acceleration error ddotq_NN - ddotq_ref  ddotq_ref of a learned Lagrangian, over the columns of x and of q̈_ref (Offen 2025, §7.3).\n\nx is the state q dotq of each sample, a 2ntimesB matrix, and q̈_ref the reference acceleration, an ntimesB matrix. ddotq_NN is the acceleration the Euler– Lagrange equations of the network predict, (nabla_dotqnabla_dotqL)^-1(nabla_qL - (nabla_qnabla_dotqL)^Tdotq), which is lagrangian_acceleration; both norms are those of the whole ntimesB array.\n\nThe relative error is invariant under the gauge L mapsto rho L + c + fracddtF(q) of the learned Lagrangian, because the acceleration is: rho cancels between the inverse velocity Hessian and the gradients, and c and the total derivative drop out of them. Scaling the reference changes the error. model is a NeuralNetwork of a LagrangianArchitecture, and one of a DiscreteLagrangianArchitecture throws an ArgumentError. x of the wrong row count, and an acceleration of the wrong row or column count, throw a DimensionMismatch; no columns, and a q̈_ref whose norm is zero, throw an ArgumentError; and a singular nabla_dotqnabla_dotqL throws a LinearAlgebra.SingularException.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.acceleration_error"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.energy_alignment","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"energy_alignment(E, E_ref, points)\n\nThe mean over the columns x of points of sintheta, the angle between nabla E(x) and nabla E_ref(x). It is 0 where the two energies single out the same level sets at that point, whose gradients are then parallel, and 1 where those gradients are perpendicular.\n\nThis is the measure nu of Ober-Blöbaum & Offen 2023, §3.1, the mean of det(nabla E_refnabla E_ref nabla Enabla E), which their two-dimensional benchmarks use and which generalises to a state of 2n  2 entries as the sine of the angle. It is invariant under the gauge L mapsto rho L + c + fracddtF(q), which multiplies nabla E by the same factor rho at every point, so sintheta is unchanged.\n\npoints is a 2ntimesB matrix of states. Each of E and E_ref is the result of energy, whose gradient is used, or a function x ↦ ∇E(x) of a state that returns the gradient of length 2n: the energy of a benchmark has a closed-form gradient, and no dependency of this package differentiates an arbitrary function.\n\nA point whose gradient is exactly zero for either energy has no direction, and is left out of the mean; when every point is, and when points has no column, an ArgumentError is thrown. The value has the element type of the gradients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.energy_alignment"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.trajectory_error","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"trajectory_error(Q, Q_ref)\n\nThe largest cdot_2 distance between the columns of two trajectories, Q and Q_ref, each an ntimesk matrix whose column k is the position at step k.\n\nIt is the error of a rollout against a reference solution — the measure of a learned model that a variational integrator needs, since a trajectory is what the model predicts and a position is what it is compared at (Offen 2025, §7.3). The worst column is taken rather than a mean, because a rollout that is right everywhere but one step is a rollout that has lost the trajectory.\n\nTrajectories of different sizes throw a DimensionMismatch and empty ones an ArgumentError; a NaN in either gives NaN. The value has the element type of the positions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.trajectory_error"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"M. Cranmer, S. Greydanus, S. Hoyer, P. Battaglia, D. Spergel and S. Ho. Lagrangian Neural Networks (2020), arXiv:2003.04630 [cs.LG].\n\n\n\nA. U. Hamzaogullari and A. Ozakin. Learning Relativistic Geodesics and Chaotic Dynamics via Stabilized Lagrangian Neural Networks (2026), arXiv:2601.12519 [cs.LG].\n\n\n\nM. Lutter, C. Ritter and J. Peters. Deep Lagrangian Networks: Using Physics as Model Prior for Deep Learning (2019), arXiv:1907.04490 [cs.LG].\n\n\n\nC. Offen. Machine learning of continuous and discrete variational ODEs with convergence guarantee and uncertainty quantification. Mathematics of Computation 95, 2339–2386 (2025).\n\n\n\nS. Ober-Blöbaum and C. Offen. Variational learning of Euler–Lagrange dynamics from data. Journal of Computational and Applied Mathematics 421, 114780 (2023).\n\n\n\nC. Offen and S. Ober-Blöbaum. Learning Discrete Lagrangians for Variational PDEs from Data and Detection of Travelling Waves. In: Geometric Science of Information (GSI 2023), Lecture Notes in Computer Science (Springer, Cham, 2023); pp. 569–579, arXiv:2302.08232.\n\n\n\nC. Offen and S. Ober-Blöbaum. Learning of discrete models of variational PDEs from data. Chaos 34 (2024).\n\n\n\nY. Lishkova, P. Scherer, S. Ridderbusch, M. Jamnik, P. Liò, S. Ober-Blöbaum and C. Offen. Discrete Lagrangian Neural Networks with Automatic Symmetry Discovery. IFAC-PapersOnLine 56, 3203–3210 (2023), arXiv:2211.10830.\n\n\n\n","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.jl","page":"Home","text":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks learns a scalar Lagrangian L(q dotq) with a neural network, and takes the dynamics from that Lagrangian through the Euler–Lagrange equations.\n\nIt exports the architectures LagrangianNeuralNetwork and DiscreteLagrangianNetwork, the supertype LagrangianArchitecture, the default activation softplus, the wrapper NormalisedLagrangian, which fixes the value and the momentum of a Lagrangian at one point, and four losses: LNNLoss, which compares the acceleration that the learned Lagrangian predicts against accelerations in the data, EulerLagrangeLoss, which asks the learned Lagrangian to satisfy the Euler–Lagrange equations on the same data with no solve, VariationalMidpointLoss, which needs positions only, and DiscreteEulerLagrangeLoss, which trains a discrete Lagrangian on positions through the discrete Euler–Lagrange equations. inverse_modified_lagrangian reads the continuous Lagrangian that a discrete one stands for. The discrete network's own supertype, DiscreteLagrangianArchitecture, is documented and not exported. RegularisedLoss adds weighted penalties to any of them: the regularity penalties VelocityHessianRegulariser and MixedHessianRegulariser, and the scale conditions ScaleCondition and DiscreteScaleCondition. The training itself — the data loader, the batches, the optimiser and train! — is GeometricMachineLearning's.\n\nusing LagrangianNeuralNetworks\nusing GeometricMachineLearning: NeuralNetwork\n\narch = LagrangianNeuralNetwork(2; width = 4, nhidden = 1)\nnn = NeuralNetwork(arch)\nloss = LNNLoss(arch)\n\nThe page Lagrangian Neural Network describes each method with its source, and documents the functions. The tutorial trains four models of the harmonic oscillator, two on accelerations and two on positions only, and compares them through measures that do not depend on which of the many equivalent Lagrangians a network learns. The references list the sources of the methods.","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.jl"}]
}
